course: politics
lesson: 18
title: Prices, money and jobs: why everything costs more
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Last year your sweets cost 50p; now they cost 65p. Nobody stole your 15p: when the prices of most things rise over time, it is called inflation, and the same coin simply buys less.
explain: It was reported that in 2022 and 2023 prices rose fast in many countries, partly because energy and food got dearer after the pandemic and after Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine cut gas supplies to Europe.
explain: Every fix has a cost for someone. Higher interest rates calm prices but raise house payments; tariffs may protect some factory jobs but can raise prices in shops; a higher minimum wage helps workers but costs businesses more. That is why grown-ups argue about it so much.
explain: Most politicians want people to be better off; they disagree about the way: some want lower taxes so people keep more money, others want more spending to help families, and many want a mix.
explain: People grumble about prices in their own words: a Canadian says a coffee "costs a loonie more" (a loonie is a one-dollar coin) and orders a "double-double" (coffee with two creams and two sugars), a Kiwi says the price rise is "not sweet as" (not fine) and "chur" means thanks, and in the Black Country "bostin'" means brilliant.
fact: Inflation is when the prices of most things rise over time; the cost of living is how much money you need for food, heating, rent and other everyday things.
fact: Some say governments spent and printed too much money; others say world problems like the pandemic and war pushed up fuel and food costs.
fact: It was reported that energy prices shot up in 2022, especially in Europe; some governments helped with bills, and in Britain a price cap limits how much energy companies can charge.
fact: Central banks, like the Bank of England or the Federal Reserve, can raise interest rates, the extra you pay for borrowing money, to slow inflation: borrowing gets dearer, so people spend less, and families paying back a mortgage, a big loan for a house, pay more each month.
fact: A tariff is a tax on goods coming from abroad; news reports said that in 2025 the US announced many new tariffs, and some countries answered with tariffs of their own.
fact: Some say tariffs protect home factories and bring jobs back; others say the cost is often passed on to shoppers as higher prices.
fact: The minimum wage is the lowest pay per hour the law allows; raising it gives workers more but raises costs for businesses.
fact: It was reported that many nurses, teachers and rail staff went on strike in recent years because pay was not keeping up with prices; a union speaks for workers and bargains with bosses.
fact: Shrinkflation is when a product gets smaller but the price stays the same; the unit price, such as the price per 100 grams, lets you compare.
fact: News reports said more families, including some with jobs, used food banks during the cost of living crisis; using a food bank is nothing to be ashamed of.
fact: It was reported that rents and house prices have risen faster than pay in places like the UK, Ireland, Australia and Canada; some want many more homes built, others want green spaces protected.
fact: Taxes pay for things like schools and roads, benefits are money the government gives to people who need help, and a budget is the government's plan for raising and spending money.
fact: Some say lower taxes leave people freer and help businesses create jobs; others say more public spending helps those struggling most; many want a mix.
exercise: What is inflation? | When the prices of most things rise over time | When the prices of most things fall every year | When pay always doubles each spring | When shops must give some money back
exercise: What is the cost of living? | How much money you need for everyday things like food and rent | How much money the government spends each year on roads, schools and hospitals | How much one big treat costs on your birthday | How long people live in a country, on average
exercise: What was reported to have pushed up energy prices in Europe in 2022? | Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine cutting gas supplies | A very hot summer, and nothing else at all | Everyone in Europe buying new TVs at once | Banks closing on Sundays and holidays across Europe
exercise: What is a price cap on energy? | A limit on how much energy companies can charge | A tax that families pay when they put solar panels on the roof | A free gas bill sent to every home each month | A rule that energy firms must close at night
exercise: Why do central banks raise interest rates? | To slow down inflation by making borrowing dearer | To make every price in the shops fall at once | To give every family free money to spend | To pay for more houses, new roads and new schools to be built
exercise: What is a mortgage? | A big loan to buy a house | A tax that you pay on every packet of sweets | A savings account that children open at school | A pay rise you get for working a long time
exercise: What is an interest rate? | The extra you pay for borrowing money | The price of a loaf of bread | A tax on toys | A list of shop prices
exercise: What is a tariff? | A tax on goods coming from abroad | A discount you get in a shop sale | A pay rise for workers in factories | A ticket for a long train journey
exercise: What do supporters of tariffs say? | They protect home factories and bring jobs back | They make every import from abroad free for shoppers | They lower the taxes paid by every family | They end all trade between countries forever
exercise: What is the minimum wage? | The lowest pay per hour the law allows | The highest pay that anyone is allowed to get | The pocket money children get for chores | The pay a boss chooses on his own each week
exercise: Why might a café owner raise sandwich prices after the minimum wage goes up? | His costs for staff go up | The law says every café must raise prices | The bread he buys suddenly gets cheaper | His customers write and ask him to do it
exercise: Why did many nurses, teachers and rail staff strike, as reported? | Their pay was not keeping up with rising prices | They wanted longer summer holidays and nothing more | They were ordered to strike by the King himself | They were bored at work and wanted a day off
exercise: What is a union? | A group that speaks for workers and bargains with bosses | A government department that sets everyone's pay each year | A shop owned and run by the workers who use it | A bank where children keep their pocket money
exercise: What is shrinkflation? | A product gets smaller while the price stays the same | Prices of most things in the shops fall a little every week | Clothes that shrink when you wash them too hot | A shop moves into a smaller building to save rent
exercise: How can you compare shrinking packets fairly? | Look at the unit price, like the price per 100 grams | Look at which box is biggest on the shelf | Look at which packet has the brightest colours | Look at which advert on TV seemed the most fun
exercise: Why have more families with jobs used food banks, as reported? | Rent, bills and food rose faster than their wages | Food banks are only open to people who already have jobs | Shops near them stopped selling food altogether | Food banks pay families money to come along
exercise: What are benefits? | Money the government gives to people who need help | Money people pay to the government out of their wages | Free sweets and snacks handed out at every school | A cut in pay that a boss gives to lazy workers
test: Aisha: "Who stole my 15p? Sweets were 50p, now 65p!" What is the best answer? | Nobody; prices rose, so the same coin buys less | The shop owner secretly kept 15p of her change for himself | Coins slowly lose their metal and their value | The sweets got bigger, so they cost more now
test: «gas bill's mental, we're all in jumpers now, heating on at 8» What is the link to the news, as reported? | Energy prices shot up in 2022 after the war cut gas supplies to Europe | Gas got cheaper, so families chose to use less and save | A new law said everyone must wear jumpers indoors | The family's heater broke and they could not fix it
test: "The bank put rates up again, mortgage is gonna kill us" groans Noah's dad. Why would a bank do that? | To slow inflation by making borrowing dearer | To punish families who own houses with loans | To make savers poorer and borrowers richer | To make prices in the shops rise even faster
test: A dad who works at a car parts factory says tariffs will bring jobs back; the neighbour says they will make everything cost more. Which is best? | Both can be partly true: a tax at the border can help some people and cost others | Only the dad can be right, because he works in a factory and knows best | Only the neighbour can be right, because tariffs never save a single job | Neither is right, because tariffs have no effects on jobs or prices at all
test: "Minimum wage up in April, chur!" says Mia's sister in Auckland (chur = thanks, great). What might the café owner do? | Raise the price of a sandwich or hire fewer people | Pay her less than the law allows and hope nobody notices | Close the café forever, because the law now says so | Lower all his prices so more customers come in
test: Teachers strike on Tuesday, trains the next week, and Gran can't visit. "Heaps annoying" says Jake. What is the point being argued? | Strikes press for pay but are felt by everyone who uses the service | Strikes are always against the law and the strikers all go to prison for it | Teachers and train drivers just wanted a day off for fun | Strikes only hurt the bosses and never the people who use trains
test: «they SHRANK it, same price, that's not bostin'» (bostin' = brilliant). What can Chloe check? | The weight on the packet and the unit price | The colour of the wrapper and the logo | The shop's opening hours on the door | The advert for it that was on TV last night
test: A boy says the girl's family can't use the food bank because "her dad has a job". What is true? | Some families with jobs still cannot cover rising rent, bills and food | Only people without any job at all can ever be hungry | Food banks check everyone's school marks before they help | Her dad must be lying about having a job
test: Grace's cousin, 25, can't afford to move out. His mum says, "In my day we bought a house at 25!" What was reported? | Rents and house prices have risen faster than pay in many places | Houses are cheaper today than they have ever been before | Nobody at all bought a house in the past, so his mum must be wrong | Young people now earn far more than their parents ever did
test: Two politicians say "cut taxes" and "spend more". Aroha's mum says they want the same thing. What does she mean? | Both want people better off; they disagree about the way | Both secretly want prices to go up so that they earn more | Both want to get rid of the government altogether | Neither of them cares about families at all, really
test: What would happen to a family with a big mortgage if interest rates fell? | Their monthly house payment would usually go down | Their monthly house payment would usually go up a lot | Their house would get bigger by itself | Their whole mortgage would simply disappear
test: «a coffee's a loonie more than last year, eh» says a Toronto dad (loonie = one-dollar coin). What is this an example of? | Inflation | A tariff on maple syrup | Shrinkflation in the coffee cup | A strike by café workers
exam: Identify the term which denotes a sustained rise in the general level of prices over time. | Inflation | Shrinkflation | A tariff | A mortgage
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of an increase in interest rates by a central bank? | Borrowing becomes more costly, and households with mortgages pay more each month | Borrowing becomes less costly, and all prices fall immediately | Households with mortgages pay less each month, the loans being cheaper | No household is affected in any respect, since interest rates concern the banks alone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the term "tariff". | A tax levied upon goods imported from abroad | A payment made to workers by the government | A limit upon the price charged per unit of energy | A reduction in the size of a product at a constant price
exam: It is given that a product is reduced in weight whereas its price remains unchanged. Determine the measure which permits a shopper to compare its value accurately. | The unit price, such as the price per one hundred grams | The colour and design of the packaging upon the shelf | The number of advertisements broadcast for the product | The size of the box, irrespective of its contents
